WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
This episode was sponsored by Wren,&nbsp;
a website where you calculate your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.960
carbon emissions. You can also sign&nbsp;
up to make a monthly contribution to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:15.920
offset your emissions or support&nbsp;
rainforest protection projects.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.480
Tardigrades are famous for their capacity&nbsp;
to survive. If you look them up, you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.640
be inundated with long lists of the many things&nbsp;
scientists have thrown their way to see if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.640
tardigrade will survive. We’ve made some lists&nbsp;
like those ourselves. Tardigrades on the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.760
tardigrades in extreme heat. You’ve heard about&nbsp;
them. We’ve talked about them. What else is there?&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.200
Well, here’s a surprising fact you might not&nbsp;
have known. For certain species of tardigrades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.840
like the one in the middle&nbsp;
of your screen right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.200
you can tell the difference between a male&nbsp;
and a female by looking at their toes.&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.080
That’s right. The toes.
These tardigrades are called Milnesium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.120
and the males of these species have a pair of&nbsp;
claws on their feet that are shaped a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.800
differently from the rest of the claws, with only&nbsp;
one hook on each claw instead of the usual two.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.920
But after taking a closer peek&nbsp;
at this tardigrade’s toes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.920
we can confirm that she is a female tardigrade.
Now, that’s a fun fact for us as we watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.840
the clip. But it does not actually do&nbsp;
anything for the poor rotifers that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.920
surrounding this tardigrade, who are currently&nbsp;
stretching themselves in and out of danger.&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.880
Around the tip of a tardigrade’s mouth are small&nbsp;
bumps that we think act like little sensors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:51.280
And when those bumps make contact with a rotifer,&nbsp;
out pops the tardigrade’s stylet—a needle that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.960
pokes out from the tubular mouth opening.
That stylet pokes into the rotifer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.080
and from there the tardigrade uses a circular&nbsp;
structure called a pharynx in its head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:08.240
to create strong suction. And then, the stylet&nbsp;
goes from functioning as a needle to functioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.400
as a portable, built-in straw that lets the&nbsp;
tardigrade suck out the inside of its prey.&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.480
You can see some of the bodily fluid leaving&nbsp;
the rotifer as the tardigrade’s stylet pumps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:27.760
away like a beating heart. And when she is done,&nbsp;
the rotifer remains, like an empy coffee cup.&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.320
This method of eating works quite well for&nbsp;
the tardigrade, letting her eat quickly from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.320
the prey that is directly in front of her.&nbsp;
But not all organisms come equipped with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.680
weapon that lets them turn rotifers into Capri&nbsp;
Suns. These organisms have to turn to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.760
methods to extract nutrients from their food.
This marine ciliate is called kentrophyllum, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.520
it came to James, our master of microscopes, in&nbsp;
a large container full of beach sand. On a normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.880
day, maybe we would talk more about its funny&nbsp;
almond shape that stretches as it swims around.&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.400
But today is not a normal day&nbsp;
because this kentrophyllum is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.360
about to be overshadowed by a rotifer.
Now, where is the rotifer, you might ask?&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:17.920
It is inside the kentrophyllum, of course.&nbsp;
We did not catch the moment of its capture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:22.800
when the toxic needles lining the kentrophyllum&nbsp;
darted out and paralyzed the rotifer. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:31.840
time we arrived, the rotifer had already&nbsp;
been ingested…but it was not dead yet.&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.480
You can see it wiggling and wrestling&nbsp;
at the broad end of the kentrophyllum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.640
causing the ciliate’s body to wrinkle and&nbsp;
fold in on itself. But it is trapped inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:48.000
of a food vacuole now, a compartment&nbsp;
that exists to break this rotifer down.&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.560
Sure, the rotifer can try and fight&nbsp;
against the walls of the vacuole holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.760
it hostage. But there’s not much it can do&nbsp;
against the digestive enzymes pouring in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:05.040
or the increasing acidity of its surroundings.&nbsp;
A food vacuole is a hostile place to be, and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:12.080
minutes, its destructive tools will go to work.
In the end, the rotifer will be left in dissolved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.440
pieces to be absorbed by the kentrophyllum’s&nbsp;
cytoplasm, sustaining the organism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.400
was the site of its last battle.
It’s a lonely death for the rotifer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:28.320
with only the kentrophyllum to witness it.&nbsp;
Though I suppose now there is also us, sharing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:36.640
and immortalizing that rotifer’s last moments.
And if the rotifer had not died in the body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.080
of another organism, it may have&nbsp;
ended up like this gastrotrich.&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:46.080
Now to be fair, they did find themselves in&nbsp;
a very similar situation: they are both dead.&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.520
But the rotifer was eaten alive, digested from&nbsp;
the outside while trapped in another organism.&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:57.760
Meanwhile, this gastrotrich is going through the&nbsp;
exact opposite situation. It is being eaten from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:04.640
the inside out, by a scavenger called a peranema.
Now, as far as we know, the peranema didn’t do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:10.480
anything to kill the gastrotrich. It was likely&nbsp;
already dead, though we don’t know what killed it.&nbsp;

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:16.000
But the peranema is both a hunter and a scavenger.&nbsp;
Sometimes it can be found with other peranema as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:22.960
they hunt down prey, in a pack of hungry microbes.
At other times, the peranema scavenges. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.160
are notorious for being able to&nbsp;
squeeze their way into whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:32.160
holes they find to get into a dead organism.
And in this case, the gastrotrich’s exoskeleton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:38.560
was the right combination of available, open,&nbsp;
and dead for the peranema, which found its way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:44.640
in and decided to begin helping itself to the&nbsp;
buffet of dead tissue around it. After all, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:50.080
gastrotrich won’t be needing that tissue anymore,&nbsp;
so the peranema might as well make good use of it.&nbsp;

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.160
The gastrotrich’s body has likely vanished&nbsp;
by now. Perhaps the peranema will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.560
finished it off entirely, or some other&nbsp;
scavenger will have joined in on the fun.&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.240
Or perhaps, it will have simply faded,&nbsp;
like the rotifer inside the kentrophyllum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.480
its body eventually fading into&nbsp;
the world that encapsulates it.&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.800
But for this last moment, let’s remember&nbsp;
the gastrotrich in a more beautiful moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:22.800
under a fluorescent light, glowing in the&nbsp;
glorious purple autofluorescence of its remains.&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:37.280
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

